From 09944114a67f3d4ccf01d12f51812fc31ed218bd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Deniz Kokden <koekdendeniz@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 22:09:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 022/109] ARM: sprd: SC7730 SMP: enter secondary_startup_arm in
 ARM state

The boot ROM jumps to JMP_ADDR with interworking. In a Thumb-2 kernel
built with clang, the LLVM assembler marks secondary_startup_arm as a
Thumb function (its ENDPROC comes after the switch to Thumb), so its
address carries bit 0: the secondaries started decoding the ARM entry
code as Thumb, ran wild and froze the system. GNU as leaves bit 0 clear,
which is why the GCC build worked. Clear it explicitly.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
Signed-off-by: Deniz Kokden <koekdendeniz@gmail.com>
---
 arch/arm/mach-sprd/platsmp.c | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm/mach-sprd/platsmp.c b/arch/arm/mach-sprd/platsmp.c
index f6b542667..0235b76eb 100644
--- a/arch/arm/mach-sprd/platsmp.c
+++ b/arch/arm/mach-sprd/platsmp.c
@@ -67,7 +67,15 @@ static int sc7730_boot_secondary(unsigned int cpu, struct task_struct *idle)
 		return -ENODEV;
 
 	spin_lock(&sc7730_smp_lock);
-	writel(__pa_symbol(secondary_startup_arm), ap_ahb + AHB_JMP_ADDR(n));
+	/*
+	 * The ROM jumps with interworking, and secondary_startup_arm must be
+	 * entered in ARM state. The LLVM assembler marks it as a Thumb
+	 * function in a Thumb-2 kernel (its ENDPROC comes after the switch
+	 * to Thumb), so the address would carry bit 0 and the core would
+	 * start decoding ARM code as Thumb.
+	 */
+	writel(__pa_symbol(secondary_startup_arm) & ~1UL,
+	       ap_ahb + AHB_JMP_ADDR(n));
 	writel(readl(ap_ahb + AHB_HOLDING_PEN) | BIT(n),
 	       ap_ahb + AHB_HOLDING_PEN);
 	writel(readl(ap_ahb + AHB_CA7_RST_SET) | BIT(n),
